          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Boxes</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these boxes. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages, Amazon
            listings, warranty pages, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on this
            page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We looked for a box of each main type — soft with a firm core, all-foam, plain wood and
            adjustable steel — that was listed and in stock on Amazon US on 19 October 2026 and that we
            could cross-check. Garage Gym Reviews' plyo box roundup and its reviews of REP's soft boxes
            were the main independent sources; the Amazon listings were the second check on sizes,
            capacities, prices and sellers. We favored boxes from makers with their own store and
            published specifications, and carded Yes4All, which we could check only on Amazon, as the
            budget soft option with its figures clearly labelled.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The recall check</h3>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page — REP Fitness, Titan Fitness and Yes4All — and every brand we
            considered was searched in the CPSC's recall database on 19 October 2026, by name and with
            the words plyo, plyometric and jump box. We found no recall of any plyo box from any brand.
            REP's one recall record (2021) concerns strap safety brackets for racks, and the Titan
            recall alert we found (2018) concerns weightlifting safety straps; neither is a box. We found
            nothing for Yes4All.
          </p>
