        <div id="foam-vs-wood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Foam vs Wood vs Steel: Which Plyo Box Material</h2>
<p>
            The material decides how the box feels to land on, what happens when you miss, and how long
            it lasts.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Soft box with a wood core</h3>
          <p>
            A plywood box wrapped in foam and a textured vinyl cover, like REP's 3-in-1 Soft box and the
            Yes4All. The core keeps it stable and heavy enough not to tip; the padding means a clipped
            shin hits foam rather than a plywood edge. Garage Gym Reviews calls this combination the
            best balance of stability and safety it found. The trade-offs are cost, weight and a slightly
            softer landing than bare wood. <strong>Best for</strong> most home gyms, beginners and anyone
            doing high-rep jumps when tired.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">All-foam boxes</h3>
          <p>
            Dense foam throughout, covered in vinyl, often designed to stack and strap together, like
            REP's stackable boxes and Rogue's. They are the most forgiving to miss and the easiest to
            build into custom heights. They also compress more underfoot, can feel less stable when
            stacked high, and Garage Gym Reviews found foam boxes can begin to sag with heavy use.
            <strong>Best for</strong> people nervous about jumping, rehab-style step work under guidance,
            and gyms that want many heights.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plain wood</h3>
          <p>
            Plywood, usually flat-packed and screwed together, like Titan's Pro-Duty box. It is the
            firmest landing and the cheapest per size, and a well-built one lasts for years. It is also
            unforgiving: a missed jump drags your shin down a hard edge. Look for rounded or sanded edges
            and a non-slip top. <strong>Best for</strong> experienced jumpers, step-ups and box squats,
            and tight budgets.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Adjustable steel</h3>
          <p>
            A steel frame on telescoping legs, like REP's Adjustable Plyo Box. Its advantage over a
            3-in-1 box is that the landing area stays the same size at every height — Garage Gym Reviews
            points out that a turned-over 3-in-1 box changes its top surface, which can upset rhythm on
            box jump overs. Steel edges are hard, so the same shin caution applies as for wood.
            <strong>Best for</strong> people who change height often and want a consistent target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Which Height to Buy</h2>
<p>
            Height is where most people over-buy. Box jumps train how fast you leave the floor, not how
            high you can tuck your knees, and a box that is too tall turns a power drill into a test of
            hip mobility.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Beginners</strong> are usually best served by 12 to 20 inches. Garage Gym Reviews
              suggests most novices begin at 16 inches and some at 20. Start lower than you think, land
              softly and stand tall at the top.</li>
            <li><strong>Regular trainees</strong> mostly use 20 and 24 inches, the heights many
              group-fitness workouts prescribe. A 20 x 24 x 30 in. 3-in-1 box covers that range with one
              step up.</li>
            <li><strong>Step-ups, split squats and box squats</strong> generally want a lower box than
              jumping — roughly knee height or a little below for step-ups, which for many adults is 16
              to 20 inches.</li>
            <li><strong>Stacked foam</strong> lets you build odd heights, but a tall stack is less stable.
              Garage Gym Reviews' tester had no problems at 36 inches and under but found stacks above
              that could sway on landing.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Check the ceiling</h3>
          <p>
            This is the check people forget. Standing on top of a 30-inch box, a six-foot person's head
            is at 102 inches — eight feet six inches — which is above a standard eight-foot ceiling,
            and the jump itself goes higher. On a 20-inch box the same person's head is at 92 inches.
            Measure your ceiling, any beams, lights and garage door tracks, and choose the box height
            accordingly. A basement or spare room is often where a 24 or 30-inch box stops making sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stability-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Stability and Landing Safety (Including Your Shins)</h2>
<p>
            Most box injuries come from two things: a miss on the way up, and a hard landing on the way
            down. A few habits and the right setup prevent most of both.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Put the box on a grippy floor.</strong> REP and Titan both say not to use their
              boxes on smooth or slippery surfaces, and Garage Gym Reviews reports Rogue says the same of its foam boxes. Rubber
              flooring under the box keeps it planted and protects the floor; our
              <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
              covers the options.</li>
            <li><strong>Land in the center.</strong> Titan's own note is to always land in the middle
              of the box. Edge landings are what make light boxes tip.</li>
            <li><strong>Step down, do not jump down.</strong> Jumping off the box adds a landing from
              full height that does little for training. Stepping down, one foot at a time, is the
              common coaching advice, especially for beginners and in high-rep workouts.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect your shins.</strong> A soft box is the simplest protection. On wood or
              steel, start lower than your limit, keep sessions short, and stop when jumps get
              sloppy.</li>
            <li><strong>Respect fatigue.</strong> The risky reps are the last ones in a conditioning
              workout, when feet clip the edge. Lower the box rather than chase the height you used
              when fresh.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the box itself.</strong> Tighten screws on a wood box from time to time,
              check that the pins on an adjustable box are fully seated before every session — REP's own
              review summary notes some owners say the pins need careful checking — and retire foam
              boxes whose straps or stitching have failed.</li>
            <li><strong>Clear space.</strong> Leave room on every side for a missed jump, away from
              racks, walls and weights on the floor.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If jumping is not a goal in itself, there are lower-impact ways to train power and
            conditioning. A
            <a href="/best-jump-ropes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">jump rope</a>
            builds foot speed with smaller landings, and a
            <a href="/best-rebounders/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rebounder</a>
            gives a softer bounce.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="beyond-jumps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What Else a Plyo Box Is Good For</h2>
<p>
            A box earns its space even if you never jump on it. Common uses in a home gym:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Step-ups and lateral step-ups</strong>, with or without dumbbells, at knee height
              or below.</li>
            <li><strong>Bulgarian split squats</strong>, rear foot on the box.</li>
            <li><strong>Box squats</strong>, sitting back to a set depth, which is why capacity matters:
              your bodyweight plus any load. Garage Gym Reviews makes the point that a 150 lb person doing
              150 lb hip thrusts off a box needs at least a 300 lb capacity.</li>
            <li><strong>Decline push-ups, elevated planks and dips</strong>, where a firm, non-slip top
              matters more than padding.</li>
            <li><strong>Seated work and stretching</strong>, as a sturdy seat in a gym with no bench.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For loaded work, wood and steel boxes give the firmest platform; soft boxes are fine for
            most of it but compress a little. If you will mainly use the box with dumbbells, our
            <a href="/best-dumbbell-sets/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dumbbell sets guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-adjustable-kettlebells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable kettlebell guide</a>
            cover the weights, and a
            <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">proper weight bench</a>
            is still the better seat for pressing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you train in a low-ceilinged room</strong>, a tall box may not fit at all; a 16 or
            20-inch box, or a step platform, may be the realistic choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have knee, ankle or Achilles problems</strong>, or have not trained for a long
            time, jumping is not the place to start. Get advice from a qualified professional, and build
            up with step-ups and controlled strength work first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want conditioning more than jumping</strong>, a machine may serve you better.
            Our guides to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for HIIT</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cardio machines for home</a>
            cover the options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you live above someone</strong>, repeated jumps are loud and transmit through a
            suspended floor even with a soft box. Train at reasonable hours on dense rubber, keep
            sessions short, and talk to your neighbors first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best plyo box for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home gyms, a soft box with a firm core, such as REP Fitness's 3-in-1 Soft Plyo Box, because it is stable enough to jump onto and padded enough to protect your shins on a miss. Choose REP's adjustable steel box if you want the same landing area at every height, or a plywood box like Titan's Pro-Duty if you want the firmest landing for the least money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a foam or wood plyo box better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often you might miss. Wood gives a firmer, more stable landing and costs less, but a missed jump scrapes your shins on a hard edge. Foam forgives misses and suits beginners and high-rep workouts, but compresses more underfoot and can sag with heavy use. A soft box with a wood core combines much of both.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What height plyo box should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most beginners do best between 12 and 20 inches, and many people training regularly use 20 and 24 inches. A 3-in-1 box whose middle height is the one you will use most gives you a step down and a step up. Check your ceiling too: on a 30-inch box a six-foot person's head is about eight and a half feet off the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 3-in-1 plyo box or an adjustable plyo box better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3-in-1 box is simpler, cheaper and available soft, but its top surface changes size when you turn it to a new height. An adjustable steel box keeps the same landing area at every height and changes in seconds, but usually tops out lower, around 24 inches, and has hard steel edges with no padding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you avoid shin injuries on box jumps?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a soft box, start lower than your limit, land in the center of the box, step down rather than jump down, and lower the box when you are tired, because most misses happen late in a workout. Put the box on a grippy floor and leave clear space around it. If you have joint or tendon problems, get professional advice first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight can a plyo box hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The boxes on this page list capacities of 400 lb for REP's soft and steel boxes and 440 lb for Yes4All's, as maker or listing figures. Titan's capacity varies by source. For box squats or loaded step-ups, count your bodyweight plus the load, and stay well inside the rating.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Plyo Box</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you buy, and some of them are expensive in skin.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the tallest box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 30-inch box looks like the serious choice, but most people train at 16 to 24 inches, and a box that is too tall turns jumps into a mobility test. Buy the size whose middle height you will use most, and check the ceiling before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing bare wood for high-rep workouts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wood is firm and cheap, and it is also the box that takes skin off your shins when you clip the edge late in a tired set. If your workouts include many jumps for time, a soft box is the safer buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the box on a smooth floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Makers warn against using their boxes on smooth or slippery surfaces, because the box can slide as you land. Put it on rubber flooring or a grippy mat, and land in the center.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Jumping down off the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Rebounding off the top adds a landing from full height that does little for training. Step down one foot at a time, especially when you are new to jumping or tired, and keep the effort for the jump up.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

